<title>BRAZIL: Brazil soy areas seen cold and dry this week-Somar.</title>
<headline>Brazil soy areas seen cold and dry this week-Somar.</headline>
<dateline>RIO DE JANEIRO 1997-04-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazil's key southern soybean growing regions are set to remain cold but mostly dry this week following the arrival of a mass of polar cold air in the region, independent meteorologist Somar said.</p>
<p>The weather will be sunny but cold, mainly in the early hours, until April 9 when northeasterly winds blow some warmth into the southern regions, Somar said in a weekly report.</p>
<p>A new cold front will bring rains to Rio Grande do Sul state at the weekend, it added.</p>
<p>The soybean growing areas of Mato Grosso state are forecast to be warm and sunny with temperatures above 30 degrees C. No significant rains are seen in the region during this period.</p>
<p>Average rainfall in key growing regions in milimeters were as follows:</p>
<p>				 TOTAL	     TOTAL	   AVERAGE</p>
<p>			     March 1-31    April 1-7	     April</p>
<p>Londrina (PR)		    34.2	    6.0		 100</p>
<p>Cascavel (PR)		    43.7	    2.0		  95</p>
<p>Passo Fundo (RS)		 33.2	    4.0		 128</p>
<p>Sao Luis Gonzaga (RS)	 128.0	    5.0		 177</p>
<p>Santa Maria (RS)		 19.0	    0.0		 141</p>
<p>Mato Grosso		     160.2	    9.0		 115</p>
<p>((--Joelle Diderich, Rio de Janeiro newsroom +55 21 507 4151))</p>
